WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.970
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.046
Communication is critical for

00:00:08.046 --> 00:00:09.483
our professional and

00:00:09.483 --> 00:00:10.926
our personal lives.

00:00:10.926 --> 00:00:12.564
I'm Matt Abrahams, and

00:00:12.564 --> 00:00:14.982
I'm a lecturer at Stanford Graduate

00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:16.620
School of Business, and

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.882
the host of the GSB's Think Fast,

00:00:18.882 --> 00:00:20.676
Talk Smart, The Podcast.

00:00:20.676 --> 00:00:22.980
I am so excited today to share with

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.038
you five key takeaways from my

00:00:25.038 --> 00:00:26.602
class, essentials of

00:00:26.602 --> 00:00:28.591
strategic communication.

00:00:28.591 --> 00:00:34.883
[MUSIC]

00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:37.045
When it comes to our communication,

00:00:37.045 --> 00:00:37.602
many of us

00:00:37.602 --> 00:00:39.345
make a fundamental mistake,

00:00:39.345 --> 00:00:41.170
we start from the wrong place.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:42.659
We start by saying,

00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:44.587
this is what I want to say,

00:00:44.587 --> 00:00:47.218
rather than thinking about what our

00:00:47.218 --> 00:00:49.830
audience needs to hear.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.513
We need to do reconnaissance,

00:00:51.513 --> 00:00:53.393
reflection and research to better

00:00:53.393 --> 00:00:55.534
understand our audience's knowledge

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:57.024
level, their attitudes,

00:00:57.024 --> 00:00:59.098
their resistance points, along with

00:00:59.098 --> 00:01:01.670
their expectations and motivation.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.396
By taking the time to really think

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:04.506
about our audience,

00:01:04.506 --> 00:01:06.234
we can make sure that our messages

00:01:06.234 --> 00:01:08.084
resonate, and are more likely to be

00:01:08.084 --> 00:01:09.698
remembered, and acted upon.

00:01:09.698 --> 00:01:14.274
[MUSIC]

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:16.162
Most communication needs

00:01:16.162 --> 00:01:17.705
to be goal-driven,

00:01:17.705 --> 00:01:20.627
especially strategic communication.

00:01:20.627 --> 00:01:22.629
To me a goal has three parts,

00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:25.920
information, emotion, and action.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.297
In other words, what do you want

00:01:27.297 --> 00:01:28.750
your audience to know?

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.040
How do you want them to feel?

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.426
And what do you want them to

00:01:31.426 --> 00:01:32.750
do when you're done with

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:33.960
your communication?

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.600
Having a clear goal,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.840
allows you to focus your message on

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.080
the needs of your audience, and it

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.243
allows you to judge the success of

00:01:43.243 --> 00:01:45.656
your communication after the fact.

00:01:45.656 --> 00:01:50.070
[MUSIC]

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.280
Have you recently been victimized

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.109
by somebody who just rambles

00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:54.800
on and on?

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.390
Doesn't feel so good, does it?

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:58.732
We need to make sure that we

00:01:58.732 --> 00:02:00.381
structure our information, so

00:02:00.381 --> 00:02:01.418
we make it easier for

00:02:01.418 --> 00:02:03.100
our audience to follow.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.045
In fact, our brains are wired for

00:02:05.045 --> 00:02:07.270
structured information.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.169
There are lots of structures and

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:10.646
frameworks you can use to

00:02:10.646 --> 00:02:12.543
communicate, my favorite is,

00:02:12.543 --> 00:02:14.100
three simple questions.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.010
What?

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.560
So what?

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.840
And, now what?

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:18.838
What is the information that

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:19.590
you're conveying?

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.260
It's your idea, it's your belief,

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.981
it's your, product, your service,

00:02:22.981 --> 00:02:24.680
your offering.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.241
So what is about the relevance for

00:02:26.241 --> 00:02:27.120
your audience?

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.831
What's the value that your idea of

00:02:28.831 --> 00:02:30.070
products, service, or

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.360
belief brings?

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.390
And then finally, the now what?

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:34.649
That's what's comes next,

00:02:34.649 --> 00:02:36.210
maybe you're taking questions, or

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.469
showing a demonstration, or

00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:39.400
setting up another meeting.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.401
By leveraging structure, you not

00:02:41.401 --> 00:02:43.264
only help your audience process

00:02:43.264 --> 00:02:45.472
your information more fluently, but

00:02:45.472 --> 00:02:47.266
it helps you to think about your

00:02:47.266 --> 00:02:48.922
message in a more concise and

00:02:48.922 --> 00:02:49.622
clear way.

00:02:49.622 --> 00:02:53.379
[MUSIC]

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:55.134
Attention is the most precious

00:02:55.134 --> 00:02:56.282
commodity we have in

00:02:56.282 --> 00:02:57.300
the world today.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.428
We are constantly bombarded with

00:02:59.428 --> 00:03:00.880
information.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.135
If we are to achieve our

00:03:02.135 --> 00:03:03.459
communication goal and

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:05.378
help our audience with the content

00:03:05.378 --> 00:03:07.429
we're giving, we must take the time

00:03:07.429 --> 00:03:08.870
to engage them.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.430
To me engagement is just

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.470
sustained attention.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:13.796
And there are three engagement

00:03:13.796 --> 00:03:14.867
techniques I'd like for

00:03:14.867 --> 00:03:16.330
you to consider using.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.152
The first is physical engagement,

00:03:18.152 --> 00:03:19.549
where you get your audience

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:21.007
actually doing something,

00:03:21.007 --> 00:03:22.160
responding to a poll,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.314
typing into the chat,

00:03:23.314 --> 00:03:25.650
collaborating on a whiteboard.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.868
The second is cognitive engagement,

00:03:27.868 --> 00:03:30.017
getting people answering questions

00:03:30.017 --> 00:03:32.270
or considering an analogy.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.703
And the third is linguistic,

00:03:34.703 --> 00:03:36.357
using the word you or

00:03:36.357 --> 00:03:39.188
people's names, draws them in.

00:03:39.188 --> 00:03:41.392
Using terms like picture this, or

00:03:41.392 --> 00:03:43.368
imagine, or think back to when

00:03:43.368 --> 00:03:45.192
are ways that you can get your

00:03:45.192 --> 00:03:47.473
audience to visualize what you're

00:03:47.473 --> 00:03:48.626
communicating.

00:03:48.626 --> 00:03:50.491
Using engagement techniques,

00:03:50.491 --> 00:03:52.563
help enliven your communication,

00:03:52.563 --> 00:03:54.151
and they help your audience

00:03:54.151 --> 00:03:55.610
to engage and remember.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:00.099
[MUSIC].

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:02.710
Finally, even if we have the most

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.603
audience centric, goal directed,

00:04:05.603 --> 00:04:06.817
structured and

00:04:06.817 --> 00:04:09.803
engaging communication, we can fall

00:04:09.803 --> 00:04:12.713
flat if our presence isn't strong.

00:04:12.713 --> 00:04:14.841
Having an authentic, confident

00:04:14.841 --> 00:04:16.589
presence in your writing or

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.109
speaking is critical to

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:20.750
your communication success.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:22.200
If you're presenting,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.140
record yourself and watch.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.244
If you're writing,

00:04:25.244 --> 00:04:27.023
proofread what you've read, and

00:04:27.023 --> 00:04:28.312
certainly ask others for

00:04:28.312 --> 00:04:30.220
feedback to help you.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.079
Communication is critical to your

00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:33.140
success.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.138
Using these takeaways, can help you

00:04:35.138 --> 00:04:37.320
improve your communication.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.787
We must take the time to

00:04:38.787 --> 00:04:40.718
dedicate to this improvement

00:04:40.718 --> 00:04:43.191
through repetition, reflection and

00:04:43.191 --> 00:04:45.507
feedback, but doing so, will help

00:04:45.507 --> 00:04:47.376
you get your points across.

00:04:47.376 --> 00:04:57.376
[MUSIC]

00:05:02.722 --> 00:05:04.240
I never had a problem talking, so

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.705
I don't think people who knew me

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:07.115
would be surprised that I'm in

00:05:07.115 --> 00:05:08.470
a role where I'm doing a lot of

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:09.030
speaking.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.340
But I thought I was going to be

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.008
a doctor, and then I met calculus

00:05:12.008 --> 00:05:13.498
and we didn't get along, and

00:05:13.498 --> 00:05:14.690
then I met chemistry and

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:15.955
we got along even worse.

00:05:15.955 --> 00:05:18.459
So, I found communication as my

00:05:18.459 --> 00:05:19.302
passion.

00:05:19.302 --> 00:05:20.978
So, I finally found the subject

00:05:20.978 --> 00:05:22.281
that started with a C that

00:05:22.281 --> 00:05:23.095
